] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125489 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125490 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Potential Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125489 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050875 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186124746 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3a675a3e-a62b-5b11-9c31-522dea85c72d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406726 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81739" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125235 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7faa34ab-beed-5e97-be56-8c5b15402d1e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125235 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡z+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125489 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125490 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125489 #edb/ref 1061 25881 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125489 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "063b4918-369c-5e01-892c-07d82af9b650" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125489 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125821 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1061 25882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c64d2d4c-15fd-55c0-90c6-c2215440ba8d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124575 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125500 #edb/ref 1061 26444 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125500 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "65be1eef-6324-59ea-bbe6-d93f3c5b9154" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125500 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124580 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\ \n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1065 4256 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3d2fa632-58e8-5d68-a8e2-fb8822d211c0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263152 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y)=2y{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263152 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263152 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "0ff13513-2c62-551f-b51b-384eba917a5e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263638 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263638 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263638 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "496b60d9-0d25-59bd-9c19-6467809cfe4a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263864 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+y+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263864 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263864 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "63500018-57ff-5df1-bcb6-85380b2c7d02" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263939 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y)=y{e}^{y}+x{y}^{2}+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263939 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263939 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "6c452c58-6b42-5c49-8585-0f39c63c429b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264522 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y}+y+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264522 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264522 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "affded30-c6e0-5d42-b054-9af00e3bf815" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263152 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263638 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263864 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263939 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264522 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186263638 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265519 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "944f1edc-f0d0-51d2-983f-32b6ee33f7f4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265519 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132080" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266137 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+2y)\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74939473-4e44-5a23-a29d-cf0a790a9021" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186266137 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}+2y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}+{g}^{′}(y) & =x{e}^{y}+2y \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =2y \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{2}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186348644 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y,z)=xz{e}^{xz}+xz+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186348644 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186348644 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "16c1bf68-0a65-5287-b32f-c0e4dc3478f2" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186348703 #edb/ref 1081 "f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3z)+C" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186348703 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186348703 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "1cca9c71-9e8d-570c-89