17592186406697 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406725 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "c4e00321-0365-5097-9e12-24d700cbbfb7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406691 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406694 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406699 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406714 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406717 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186406691 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406726 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "f7b902c9-1d56-5de9-9587-969c0111a4bb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406722 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81755" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406727 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2x{y}^{3}z\\,i+(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2})\\,j+({x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz)\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "327918f1-3cba-54fe-8e7b-c743d7b1d3cf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406727 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3}z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x{y}^{3}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int {z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz+{h}^{′}(z) & ={x}^{2}{y}^{3}+2yz \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}z+y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406721 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132084" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406728 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98240cfd-67f4-53e3-a23f-45405606e2e7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406728 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406724 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124731" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406729 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y)\\,i+(2y+x+z)\\,j+(y-2z)\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a894d767-b55b-50b3-ac91-79c05f30a84d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406729 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y+x+z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+x+z \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2y+z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2y+z)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}+yz+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2z & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy+{y}^{2}+yz-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406730 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406723 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406730 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81736" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406730 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406730 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\text{ }i+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x)\\text{ }j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406730 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3be742e-e07b-5a79-9e12-1ef7df6cb24a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406730 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406731 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406725 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406731 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37343" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406731 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406731 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+3{y}^{2})\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406731 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6e8edea-70b0-524c-abbc-330734c9a85f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406731 #edb/ref 1074 "The gradient of the functoin $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ g(y) & ={y}^{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)={y}^{3}+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}+C.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
